A||N||7||Monkeyman||Adobe ImageReady||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||09-01-2001||10:18 PM||||What is it and how do I use it?  I was playing around with Photoshop today at work (hey, if I gotta be there, I might as well have some fun) and accidentally clicked something that brought my image there.  I could close it out, but I want to know if I can do something with it 1st.  Jim, Steve, Dale, Cliff, others? <P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||09-02-2001||01:43 AM||||I used to use it until they added the same capabilities into newer version of Photoshop... All if does is give you ways to make images better suited for the web... As long as you have Photoshop 5.5 or better you don't really need it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sloth85GT||09-02-2001||04:37 AM||||It does WAYYYYY more than that Songman. Basically it's something called a WYSIWYG (pronounced wizzywig). It stands for What You See Is What You Get. It can write HTML and javascript code for you. If you want to make a web page with rollovers or animated GIF's then Imageready can do it. You use something called the slice tool to make little squares, and what those are is little images that you web page will be made up of. <P>So all in all, it can creat web pages with some added perks like as I said, rollovers. For an example of what little thing I did in Imageready, go to a web site that I designed and created, <A HREF="http://www.dhook.com" TARGET=_blank>Dr. Hook Towing</A>. The navigation bar on the left hand side was done in Imageready with rollovers. There are some slight differences in the versions of image ready and version 6 would be the best. I know that program pretty well so if you want to know how to do something, just ask and I'll help out as much as I can.<P>And to correct you again Songman, you can optimize images directly in Photoshop and you don't have to use Imageready. You just choose File--&gt;Save for Web. Then it'll save it as a whatever file you choose, but you get to play with options to make the file smaller with previews of what it'll look like. <P>Adobe rocks!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">  <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT<P>WITH THE NEW WHEELS!!!!! WOOHOO!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||09-02-2001||10:22 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sloth85GT:<BR><B>It does WAYYYYY more than that Songman. Basically it's something called a WYSIWYG (pronounced wizzywig). It stands for What You See Is What You Get. It can write HTML and javascript code for you. If you want to make a web page with rollovers or animated GIF's then Imageready can do it. You use something called the slice tool to make little squares, and what those are is little images that you web page will be made up of. <P>So all in all, it can creat web pages with some added perks like as I said, rollovers. For an example of what little thing I did in Imageready, go to a web site that I designed and created, <A HREF="http://www.dhook.com" TARGET=_blank>Dr. Hook Towing</A>. The navigation bar on the left hand side was done in Imageready with rollovers. There are some slight differences in the versions of image ready and version 6 would be the best. I know that program pretty well so if you want to know how to do something, just ask and I'll help out as much as I can.<P>And to correct you again Songman, you can optimize images directly in Photoshop and you don't have to use Imageready. You just choose File--&gt;Save for Web. Then it'll save it as a whatever file you choose, but you get to play with options to make the file smaller with previews of what it'll look like. <P>Adobe rocks!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Are you sure you aren't talking about ImageStyler for creating web pages. That is what that program does.... If ImageReady does it too then I stand corrected... Adobe does tend to give their different programs some of the same capabilities. Personally, I use Photoshop and GoLive and that is pretty much all I need for my sites, although I have all of the other programs too but just get all of the capabilities I need from these two so don't bother with the others anymore.<P>And saying that Photoshop did that was exactly what I said, so that is not really a correction... just a repeat of what I had said...<P>Nice web page, by the way... You can check out one of mine at <A HREF="http://www.dalehouston.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dalehouston.com</A>  to see what I did with Photoshop and GoLive.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||09-02-2001||10:27 AM||||Oops...<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 09-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||sloth85GT||09-02-2001||08:49 PM||||No I'm talking about Imageready, I have never heard of Image Styler. Imageready isn't photoshop, it's seperate but yet joined if that makes any sense, but yeah. <P>And nice web page too, I didn't have that high of a budget to work with for Dr. Hook so it's kinda lame, but it's functional at least and the owner lets me use his digital camera every now and then to upload the auction pictures so it's not a total loss.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Songman||09-02-2001||10:10 PM||||ImageStyler does exactly what you described as well. I didn't realize Imageready did it. I got hooked on my favorite tools and just stuck with them.<P>Thanks for the compliments on my site. My budget was zero! hehe... but since it is my own site I stick as many goodies in it as I can, hopefully without making it tacky.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
